v3.0 — THE SCRIPT IS NOW EXECUTED, NOT ONLY ANALYSED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every check so far has been static, or has tested pieces of logic copied out of
the file. None of them has ever run the program. This release adds that.

1. A HARNESS THAT RUNS THE REAL THING. A stub Roblox API, the actual script,
   and a two-line epilogue, run under Luau. The hub is driven by pulsing its
   own RunService.Heartbeat exactly as the engine would, against a base holding
   the whole loop: an order panel with both a queue counter and a lifetime
   total, a photo table with a proximity prompt, a cash pad, a pack station with
   a click detector, and a camera tool. It reports the self-check result, the
   base, the scan count, the targets found, the errors the hub recorded, and
   every action it took.

   It found three things that no static check had:

2. A REAL BUG IN THE CLOCK. With a wall clock unavailable, the hub fell through
   to os.clock() - the one time source its own comment says must never be used,
   because it measures CPU time. Every cooldown then runs slow and the hub
   appears to do nothing at all, with no error and no explanation. That is the
   worst possible failure: silent, total, and easy to blame on the game. The
   clock now has four sources, and when none of them work it says so in the log
   instead of quietly running wrong.

3. THE STUB'S OWN FAULTS, which are worth recording because each one looked
   like a hub bug at first: task.wait must yield or the keep-alive loop hangs
   the run; MoveTo must accept a Vector3 and not just a CFrame or the character
   stands still for the entire run; Magnitude and .Y are properties, not
   methods; Instance signals have to exist before anything connects to them.

4. verify.sh NOW RUNS IT, alongside the other checks, and also MEASURES the
   script's remaining register headroom by asking the compiler. It has 8 spare
   top-level locals, and the comment in the file now says so - this is not
   something to rediscover the hard way.

The harness does not prove clicks land in the live game: every input primitive
in the stub is a no-op, so confirmations and money cannot be observed there. It
proves the program runs. Those are different claims and the file says which is
which.

v2.9 — THE HUB NOW SAYS WHAT IS BROKEN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing in this release is a new feature. Every release so far has shipped with
a defect that was only found later, and the recurring one is a function using a
local declared LATER in the file: Lua resolves that as a nil GLOBAL, so it
compiles cleanly, raises nothing at load, and the feature simply stops
existing. It is invisible in review. It shipped. It survived being reviewed
three times, and was caught only because a test happened to touch that path.

So the bug class gets checked instead of hunted.

1. A SELF-CHECK THAT RUNS AT LOAD. All 129 functions the hub depends on are
   listed by reference. Building that table resolves every name immediately, so
   a name that failed to resolve appears as a missing entry and is reported by
   name - in the log on load, in the scan report, and as the FIRST line of the
   AFK diagnosis, because a build with a function missing is not "not working
   well", it is a different program from the one that was reviewed.

2. THE ANSWER GOES THROUGH STATE, NOT A CALL. The self-check has to be declared
   after every function it names, so anything that wants the result is declared
   earlier - which makes calling it from the diagnosis the exact bug it exists
   to catch. It did, immediately, on the first attempt. The result is stored and
   read, and that is now written down so the next person does not "simplify" it
   back into a call.

3. ./verify.sh - EVERY CHECK, ONE COMMAND. Both compiles, the analyzer repeated
   enough to see past its nondeterministic budget note, the global allowlist,
   the table-shape check, the hard rules (no remotes, no MarketplaceService, the
   purchase guard first in every tick and still running while paused, confirm
   clicks guarded), the self-check's coverage, and all 22 behaviour test files.
   publish.sh now runs it and refuses to publish if it fails, so it cannot be
   skipped by forgetting.

4. The 22 TEST FILES ARE IN THE REPO, not in a scratch directory. Three of them
   had drifted out of date and were passing for the wrong reason - one modelled
   matchAny with a hash instead of an array, which is precisely the bug it
   claimed to catch. A test that does not exercise the real code is worse than
   no test, because it is counted as coverage.

v2.8 — A COMPLETED TOTAL NO LONGER HIDES THE QUEUE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
v2.7 taught the hub whether a mode works. Three things stopped that working
correctly, and one of them was safety-critical.

1. "Orders Processed: 120" WAS HIDING "Orders: 3". Counters were tracked per
   BUCKET, so the larger number won and the queue was invisible. Three things
   broke at once: the planner thought work was always pending, the idle check
   never saw an empty queue, and the 3 -> 2 decrement was never seen as a
   change - so the order mode slowly talked itself into "this never works".
   Counters are now tracked per LABEL, and a completed total is filtered out
   entirely.

2. A TOTAL INCREMENTING ALONGSIDE THE QUEUE WAS COUNTED AS TWO WORKS. Processing
   one order moves both "Orders" and "Orders Processed", and crediting each
   inflated the evidence for a mode - which would have hidden a mode that was
   genuinely failing. At most one change is now credited per mode per read.

3. THE NEVER-BUY GUARD WAS DEAD CODE. matchAny() iterates with ipairs, and the
   purchase guard's word list had been written as a set (key = true), so every
   lookup iterated nothing and returned false. The guard looked correct in the
   source and did nothing at all - a check that cannot spend money, by being
   unable to recognise a purchase window. It is now a real lookup, with its own
   test covering both directions, because a guard that never fires is worse
   than no guard: it reads as protection.

   Refusing too much is its own bug, though, so matching is by WORD, split on
   case boundaries and separators: "ShopFrame" is a purchase window,
   "WorkshopFrame" is not, and "Purchase Order" - a core action in the game -
   is not either. A confirm dialog that is never clicked stays open and stalls
   the whole round.

4. A PAD NO LONGER FAKES PROGRESS. Walking onto a cash pad is credited without
   any evidence at all, so a nearby pad refreshed the "anything happening?"
   clock every three seconds for ever and the watchdog could never see a stall -
   even with photo, order and ship all dead. Pads are now counted apart from
   confirmations, and are shown apart from them in the status line. And a base
   whose only work is collecting cash no longer escalates to NEEDS ATTENTION
   for the honest reason that there is nothing verifiable to complain about.

5. TWO MORE BUGS IN THAT GUARD, both found by testing it. A word-boundary split
   was dropping the capital letter it split on, so every CamelCase name lost
   the first letter of its second word ("ShopFrame" -> "shop", "rame"). And the
   confirm-shaped buttons reached through the ordinary button path - a row
   labelled "Confirm Order" matches the order mode - were being clicked with no
   purchase check at all.

6. The per-label counter maps are pruned, so the labels the game's own UI adds
   and removes over a long session cannot grow them without limit.

v2.7 — LEARNING WHICH MODES ACTUALLY WORK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
v2.6 taught the hub how long a mode takes. This teaches it WHETHER the mode does
anything at all, which is the question the keyword lists have never been able to
answer - they say what a button is CALLED.

1. RESPONSE LEARNING. Every click is counted against its mode, and when that
   mode's own counter moves within the response window the move is credited. A
   mode clicked many times whose counter has never once moved is then treated as
   proven useless and is not clicked again - which is the difference between
   being slow and being pointless. Before this, a renamed or decoy button was
   clicked for ever and filled the log with "not responding".

   Two guards make that verdict safe:
     - a mode whose counter the observer cannot read is NEVER judged, because it
       could never earn a move in the first place;
     - a judged mode is put back on trial every three minutes, one at a time,
       so a verdict that has gone stale because the game changed costs at most
       one wasted click instead of disabling a working mode for the session.

2. IT DOES NOT WEDGE ITSELF. If every mode is ever believed useless, the hub is
   not going to be clicking anything, and it would report itself perfectly
   healthy while doing nothing. Believing that nothing works is itself the thing
   to distrust, so that case throws the verdicts away and looks again.

3. IT SAYS WHY IT STOPPED. Giving up with "something changed in the game" is
   useless. The watchdog now answers with what it measured: which modes have no
   targets, which were clicked many times without their counter moving, whether
   the base or the counters are even readable, and the last real error. Each of
   those points at a different fix.

4. A HARDER NEVER-BUY GUARD. A confirm-shaped button is not automatically safe:
   a game's own purchase window can have a button that simply reads "Confirm".
   Every confirm click is now checked against the button's own window first, and
   refused if that window mentions buying, Robux, a gamepass or a price.

5. A PUBLISH GATE FOR THE BUG CLASS THAT KEEPS BITING. A function using a local
   declared later is a silent call to a nil global - no compile error, the
   feature just dies. It shipped once while "ignore Unknown global" hid it
   among the Roblox noise. publish.sh now enforces an allowlist and refuses to
   publish anything with an unknown global that is not a Roblox or executor
   name, so that class of bug cannot be published again by accident.

v2.6 — AFK MODE, AND STOPPING THE GUESSING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Everything up to v2.5 chose timings by feel. This version measures instead, and
adds the mode you actually want: leaving it alone.

1. RESPONSE-TIME LEARNING. The flat 2.5s wait between clicks was a guess, and a
   guess is wrong in both directions: too slow for a cash pad, far too fast for
   a QC photo that needs six seconds to register. Every confirmed change now
   records how long the game actually took, and that becomes the spacing floor
   for that mode - so a slow mode stops queueing clicks on work it has already
   finished. Roughly 7 wasted clicks per 20s become 3 useful ones for a 6s
   mode, and a fast mode is not slowed down at all.

   Measuring this honestly took three attempts. The first version timed the
   hub's own settle pass, so it always read 1.2-1.7s whatever the game did. The
   second timed the settle pass and was discarded too early, so any mode slower
   than 1.2s could never be measured at all. It now measures the instant the
   game's own counter moves, and an action is judged once and KEPT afterwards
   so a late response can still be attributed to the click that caused it.

2. LEARNED TARGET QUALITY. The keyword lists say what a button is CALLED. Only
   measurement says whether it does anything. Every target now earns or loses a
   little usefulness each time it does or does not produce a change, and is
   ranked on that alongside distance. Bounded so a target that never works is
   tried rarely - never dropped.

3. IT WALKS AROUND WALLS. With teleport off, the hub walked in a straight line,
   so a fence meant pressing against it until the move timed out and the
   target was shelved for 30 seconds. It now asks the engine for a route and
   follows the waypoints (new toggle, on by default). Any failure falls straight
   back to walking straight, so the worst case is exactly the old behaviour.

4. IT ROTATES THROUGH LISTS. GUI targets were sorted biggest-first, so the same
   prominent button was clicked over and over while the rest of the order list
   was ignored. Selection now rotates, and every third pick ignores the learned
   preference entirely so a known-bad row can never become unreachable.

5. AFK MODE (F9). A watchdog that runs only while unattended: closes a dialog
   left stuck open, treats a burst of errors as corrupted cached state, repairs
   invariants that drift over a long run (cooldowns parked in the future,
   learned timings out of range, per-instance maps leaking dead parts), and
   escalates when nothing is being confirmed - rescan, then forget what it has
   learned, then report honestly instead of retrying for ever.

6. The stuck detector measures progress against the waypoint being walked AND
   the target, because either alone is wrong: waypoint-only declared a healthy
   100-stud walk stuck at six seconds, target-only declared every detour stuck.

7. Smaller: the scan report is written to a file as well as the console and now
   includes what the hub has learned; the sort no longer calls GetPivot O(n log
   n) times; unloading now stops the character walking, which Roblox otherwise
   keeps doing for several seconds after the script is gone.

v2.5 — TRUTHFUL, FAIR, AND COMPLETE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
v2.4 taught the hub to read the game. v2.5 makes sure it is honest about what
it read, fair about who it visits, and able to finish a job that takes more
than one click. Eight improvements, and every one of them closes a way the hub
could previously have lied to you or wasted its time.

1. ONLY THE CLICK THAT WORKED GETS THE CREDIT. Up to a dozen actions can be in
   flight at once, and they were ALL judged against one global question - "did
   anything on screen change at all". So one real change, or one tick of passive
   income, marked every one of them confirmed. A dead button could report itself
   as WORKING for the rest of the session. Now each action is judged on the
   evidence tied to IT: a mode whose own counter moved is proven, and everyone
   else is recorded honestly.

2. THE PLANNER'S COUNTER CREDIT IS NO LONGER EXCLUSIVE. The first version of
   the fix above credited only the first proven action per pass. But two working
   modes land in the same tick constantly, and the loser was logged as a miss -
   so a mode doing real work reported NOT RESPONDING. Each is credited on its
   own evidence. Exclusivity now applies only to the weak global rules, where
   the evidence genuinely cannot be tied to a specific click.

3. "WORKING" MEANS WORKING RIGHT NOW. A mode was called WORKING if it had EVER
   confirmed. It now reports on its most recent judged attempt, and the green
   dot in the HUD follows the same rule - so a mode that was paying out an hour
   ago and has since gone quiet turns amber instead of lying.

4. MONEY IS NOT A STATE CHANGE. The screen fingerprint included the wallet, and
   a tycoon pays out passively, so that fingerprint changed on its own several
   times a second. Wallet text is now left out whenever the wallet is readable
   some other way, so background income can no longer be mistaken for a
   successful click. Genuine counters ("Stock 123,456") are deliberately still
   included - over-filtering would drop real changes.

5. NOTHING GETS STARVED. The hub always went to the NEAREST ready target, so a
   single dead station a few studs away won every round and the photo tables on
   the far side of the plot were never visited at all. Targets are now chosen
   by a single score that rewards how long a target has been waiting and charges
   gently for distance. Nothing can be starved by a tie, and the nearest target
   still wins when everything is equally fresh.

6. EVERY MODE BACKS OFF A DEAD TARGET. Only the "fast effect" modes did, so a
   broken photo table was retried every two seconds for ever. Now all modes
   back off, with a much shorter ladder for the slow-paying ones, and a credit
   anywhere in a mode clears that mode's stale failures.

7. ORDERS CAN TAKE MORE THAN ONE CLICK. Processing an order is normally several
   steps - process, confirm, pack, confirm - and the hub used to clear exactly
   one dialog, leaving the next one stuck on screen with nothing able to close
   it. It now follows a chain of up to three, re-checking the screen between
   each, and it waits for the dialog to actually exist before believing it is
   finished.

8. IT DOES NOT CLICK WHAT IT CANNOT SEE, AND IT RECOVERS FROM A LOST BASE. A
   world target behind the player could never be clicked, because turning the
   character's body does not move the camera; the camera is now turned, the
   click made, and the camera put back (new toggle, on by default). And if the
   plot is destroyed or streamed out, the hub used to go completely silent with
   no targets and no explanation - it now notices immediately and rescans.

PLUS: a mode the game keeps reporting as empty is paused for ten seconds instead
of being clicked at for nothing, with one line in the log saying so, and it
comes straight back the moment there is work. The single most expensive repeated
UI walk in the program - the confirm scan - no longer walks the whole PlayerGui
several times a second; it reads a list collected during the walk the hub was
already making.

None of this can buy anything, fires a remote, or slows the game down. The
purchase guard still runs first on every single tick.

AUTOPILOT IN v2.4 — the hub stops guessing and starts reading
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Until now the hub was blind. It found things whose text matched a keyword and
clicked them, in a fixed priority order, hoping the game moved. v2.4 reads the
game's own state and plans the round around it.

1. THE OBSERVER. The game already shows its state as "Orders: 3" on screen.
   Every such label is now parsed into a labelled count, and the label is
   bucketed with the SAME matcher already used for targets - so the game labels
   its own state for us and not one game-specific string is hardcoded. It rides
   along on the PlayerGui walk the hub already performed, so it costs nothing.
   Counters that report a total ("Photos Taken: 500") are rejected, so a
   lifetime statistic can never be mistaken for a queue.

2. THE PLANNER. The tycoon loop is a chain: photos -> orders -> shipping ->
   cash. Instead of walking that blindly, the hub asks what the game is
   actually waiting for and gives that mode the first click of the round.
   Because the normal round still visits every enabled mode afterwards, the
   planner can only ever REORDER work, never remove it. A wrong guess spends
   one click on a mode that was going to run anyway. If the game cannot be
   read, or nothing is pending, the planner stands down and behaviour is
   exactly what it was before.

3. SELF-TUNING CADENCE. The flat wait between clicks was only ever a starting
   guess. A mode that keeps confirming is now retried faster, down to 1.2s; a
   mode that goes quiet is pulled back out again. Modes tune independently, so
   one slow corner of the map cannot hold the whole hub back - and a mode that
   can run at 1.2s now earns roughly twice the throughput it used to.

   The tuner is deliberately stricter than the verifier. Any money movement
   proves "the game reacted", but a tycoon pays out passively, so passive
   income would convince the tuner that every click worked. A cash-only
   confirmation therefore has to beat the recent rate of drift to count.

4. THE HUD now shows what the observer is reading and how fast each mode has
   tuned itself, so the state machine is visible instead of a black box.

The tuner only manages the FASTER-than-default band; slowing a mode below the
starting wait is still the existing backoff's job, so the two never fight.

FIXES IN v2.3 (found by reading the whole file end to end)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE SCAN WAS DOING HIDDEN WORK PER MODEL. Plot ownership was tested against
  every Model and Folder in the workspace on every scan, and each test
  rebuilt the player's identity and allocated a fresh attributes table. The
  identity is now computed once, the cheap name test runs first, and the
  attributes are only read for models that could actually BE a plot - a direct
  child of the workspace or of a plot container - instead of every decorative
  sub-model in the game.
* A PHOTO STATION WITH A PROMPT WAS BEING CLICKED PAST. A table whose
  ProximityPrompt sits a few studs from the part is discovered as a station,
  and the aiming path would click and never trigger the prompt. It now tries
  the prompt first, exactly like every other station.
* BUCKETS WERE STEALING EACH OTHER'S WORK. Photo's "qc" swallowed order's
  "QC Check", and collect's "claim" swallowed "Claim Daily Reward", so those
  clicks were counted and handled by the wrong mode. Specific multi-word
  phrases are now matched first - and the lowcasing is hoisted out of the
  inner loop, which had made the check two and a half times more expensive.
* CASH PADS WERE RE-MEASURED FOUR TIMES A SECOND. A base that had pads but none
  nearby set no cooldown, so the whole list was re-measured every tick, for
  ever. It is now throttled once, up front.
* EVERY RESCAN NOW GOES THROUGH ONE PLACE. Several call sites scanned the world
  directly and left the dirty flag set, so a second full workspace walk followed
  a couple of seconds later. All six now share one helper that also stops a
  scan which fails part-way from retrying four times a second for ever.
* The log keeps the ORIGINAL time on a repeated line instead of restamping it
  every time it repeats.

Design rules: client-side only — no anti-cheat bypass, no remote argument
guessing, no server manipulation. Keyword remotes are reported by SCAN but
never fired. Playing this way may violate the game's terms — your account.
